WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:01:56.420
[Music]

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.740
[Nurse Wallace:] I'm the new nurse.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:30.633
[Music]

00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:35.966
[Narrator:] And that's the way it starts. Those four 
words, "I'm your new nurse,"

00:02:35.966 --> 00:02:42.699
are your passport to a new world.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.633
Come in. You are welcome.

00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:47.833
I think I know how you feel.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:53.066
Not so long ago, I stood here, spoke the same 
words, had the same feeling.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:54.732
I know your story.

00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:57.133
You see, it's mine too.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:03.866
As a small girl with a Raggedy Ann for a patient, 
you dreamed of being a nurse.

00:03:03.866 --> 00:03:18.966
Later, when your folks agreed to let you go into 
training, the dream started to be real.

00:03:18.966 --> 00:03:27.799
And all through training, you kept your eyes set on 
this day.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.933
Soon after they handed you your diploma, you 
made a decision.

00:03:33.933 --> 00:03:38.533
Convincing your parents that it was the right thing 
to do wasn't easy.

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:40.833
They weren't too happy about it at first.

00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:44.199
"There were so many nicer jobs," they said.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.000
But your brother, Joe,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.000
he kind of understood.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.733
And Uncle Harry, the doctor, he was on your side.

00:03:51.733 --> 00:04:07.666
And that helped.

00:04:07.666 --> 00:04:11.532
So you've come to work in a tuberculosis 
sanatorium.

00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:14.599
This is the day when the dream becomes real.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.033
And I know how you feel.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:20.399
But do you have any idea how we feel about your arrival?

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.766
Here's a clue.

00:04:21.766 --> 00:04:26.599
Notice how people's eyes light up when you say, 
"I'm the new nurse."

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:31.033
Tomorrow when they take you on the big tour, 
you'll begin to catch on.

00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:41.999
Tomorrow will be a big day.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:56.700
For a moment, you'll wonder where you are.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.533
And then suddenly you'll remember.

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:05.933
A new cap is a small thing to everybody but a new 
nurse on her first job.

00:05:05.933 --> 00:05:07.166
[Woman 1:] Good morning, Miss Wallace.

00:05:07.166 --> 00:05:13.599
[Nurse Wallace:] Good morning.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:32.866
[Music]

00:05:32.866 --> 00:05:35.732
[Narrator:] You are welcome. Do you feel it?

00:05:35.733 --> 00:05:41.166
That's what everyone here is trying to tell you.

00:05:41.166 --> 00:05:53.799
Wherever you go, you'll notice that people seem 
very glad to see you.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:06:02.133
Some won't speak it, but they'll be saying it just the 
same.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:08.800
Even the little children.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.033
Everyone seems to be reaching out, toward you.

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:24.733
You don't want to get too close because you can't 
say it, but today is a day for looking.

00:06:24.733 --> 00:06:26.599
Take a look.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:42.166
Take a look at what men build for the good of their fellow men.

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:44.632
You're going to be a part of all this, Wallace.

00:06:44.633 --> 00:06:48.199
This is where you belong.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:53.166
Take a deep breath and remember it.

00:06:53.166 --> 00:06:59.199
Tomorrow you begin to work.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:08.000
And in a month of tomorrows, you'll begin to find 
out what a tuberculosis sanatorium is.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:17.200
It's washing your hands more times in one day 
than you can count.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.860
It's as many "good mornings" every morning as 
there are patients in your ward.

00:07:25.866 --> 00:07:40.799
[Music]

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:49.900
It's a hundred new things learned every day as you watch the work of other nurses.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:08:12.733
[Music]

00:08:12.733 --> 00:08:24.466
In time, you develop certain skills.

00:08:24.466 --> 00:08:38.880
You get good at handling certain things.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:48.500
But your hands don't seem to work so well when you're around a patient.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.900
This isn't just an accident.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:09:01.133
It goes deeper than that.

00:09:01.133 --> 00:09:09.099
You want to be able to work that way, but 
something always stops you.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:16.233
You keep trying.

00:09:16.233 --> 00:09:22.233
You keep failing.

00:09:22.233 --> 00:09:23.766
Why?

00:09:23.766 --> 00:09:26.899
Why are you so awkward and clumsy with patients?

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:39.500
The minute you get close to them, something happens to you, inside.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.200
What are you afraid of, Wallace?

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:49.133
[Buzzer sounds several times]

00:09:49.133 --> 00:09:53.033
It's an emergency.

00:09:53.033 --> 00:09:57.633
[Music]

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.200
Hemorrhage.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:16.360
[Dramatic music]

00:10:16.366 --> 00:10:18.399
Fix an emergency hypo. Quickly!

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.066
I have to clear his throat so he can breathe.

00:10:21.066 --> 00:11:04.899
[Dramatic music]

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:12.000
Everything's under control.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.100
That's an important word, Wallace. Control.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:19.333
Oh, I know what you're thinking.

00:11:19.333 --> 00:11:26.599
Someday soon I'll tell you how scared I was the first 
time something like this happened to me.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:31.866
I was scared of the same thing you're scared of 
and I wouldn't admit it, even to myself,

00:11:31.866 --> 00:11:33.999
for a long time.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:51.166
The fear of catching tuberculosis paralyzed me, 
just like it did you a little while ago.

00:11:51.166 --> 00:11:58.566
I won my battle with an idea.

00:11:58.566 --> 00:12:03.432
Here in this sanatorium, we work to control 
tuberculosis.

00:12:03.433 --> 00:12:10.433
And if we can control it, then we don't have to be 
afraid of it.

00:12:10.433 --> 00:12:16.333
In the meantime, there's work to do.

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:23.566
Thursdays are pneumo days.

00:12:23.566 --> 00:12:32.232
Tuesdays are operating days.

00:12:32.233 --> 00:12:40.399
Days slip into weeks, and in your ward things are going smoothly.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.633
Wallace is in control here.

00:12:44.633 --> 00:12:46.933
It's a big day for Charlie Stone.

00:12:46.933 --> 00:12:49.233
He's being transferred over to camp today.

00:12:49.233 --> 00:12:51.633
That means he's well on the way to recovery.

00:12:51.633 --> 00:13:00.966
It's a victory for both of you and you shake on it.

00:13:00.966 --> 00:13:04.632
Charlie's bed doesn't stay empty long.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:08.600
A new folder means a new patient is taking his 
place.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:34.233
[Music]

00:13:34.233 --> 00:13:37.933
[Nurse Wallace:] Hello, Ed. I'm Miss Wallace.

00:13:37.933 --> 00:13:43.466
Welcome to our ward.

00:13:43.466 --> 00:14:11.532
[Music]

00:14:11.533 --> 00:14:22.999
[Narrator:] A whole week goes by and the new patient hasn't spoken a word.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:39.833
[Music]

00:14:39.833 --> 00:14:51.999
[Doctor Sanderson:] I want to see you in your 
office, Miss Wallace.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.133
Let's move him into a sick room.

00:14:54.133 --> 00:14:57.199
He shouldn't be in the ward as long as he keeps 
this up.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.266
You've got to get him to cooperate, Wallace.

00:15:00.266 --> 00:15:03.132
The first thing is to get him to talk.

00:15:03.133 --> 00:15:06.233
Make him talk.

00:15:06.233 --> 00:15:09.099
[Nurse Wallace:] Here's another letter for you, Ed.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:15.540
Don't you want to read it?

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:41.520
[Music]

00:15:41.533 --> 00:15:48.666
Do you like the book I brought for you, Ed?

00:15:48.666 --> 00:15:55.799
Have you been reading it?

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:10.033
[Music]

00:16:10.033 --> 00:16:12.466
[Narrator:] You're ready to give up.

00:16:12.466 --> 00:16:14.532
You're thinking that there's nothing else you can do,

00:16:14.533 --> 00:16:24.533
you've tried everything.

00:16:24.533 --> 00:16:27.399
But the good doctor doesn't believe it.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:40.066
He doesn't believe you're a failure like you tell him 
you are.

00:16:40.066 --> 00:16:43.366
What was it Dr. Sanderson said?

00:16:43.366 --> 00:16:56.899
[Dr. Sanderson:] If you give up now, Wallace, he 
hasn't got a chance.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:11.266
[Narrator:] You're going to find a way. You're 
determined to find a way.

00:17:11.266 --> 00:17:17.866
You talk to Ed every day, day after day.

00:17:17.866 --> 00:17:31.699
[Music]

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:37.460
Gradually he begins to accept you, in little ways.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:50.633
[Music]

00:17:50.633 --> 00:18:03.199
You ask a hundred questions, hoping he will 
answer one of them.

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:07.600
He must answer.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:13.200
[Nurse Wallace:] Don't you want to get well? Don't 
you want to get out of here?

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.266
Why don't you fight to get well?

00:18:16.266 --> 00:18:18.966
Don't you want to get well?

00:18:18.966 --> 00:18:22.332
Why don't you act like a man and fight back?

00:18:22.333 --> 00:18:27.499
What are you going to do when you get out of 
here?

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.233
What are you going to do when you get out here?

00:18:30.233 --> 00:18:32.066
When you get out of here?

00:18:32.066 --> 00:18:34.666
When you get out of here?

00:18:34.666 --> 00:18:36.566
What are you going to do?

00:18:36.566 --> 00:18:38.232
What are going to do?

00:18:38.233 --> 00:18:40.166
What are you going to do?

00:18:40.166 --> 00:18:45.599
[Ed:] Why don't you get out of here and leave me 
alone!

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.360
[Narrator:] That's it. That's the beginning.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:59.460
[Music]

00:18:59.466 --> 00:19:02.332
From now on, it's easy.

00:19:02.333 --> 00:19:13.266
He's got a long way to go.

00:19:13.266 --> 00:19:20.032
But he's on the way.

00:19:20.033 --> 00:19:25.566
And in a different sort of way, you're on the way, 
too.

00:19:25.566 --> 00:19:34.999
[Music]

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:41.066
Your idea is that you should look into some of the 
other aspects of sanatorium nursing.

00:19:41.066 --> 00:19:45.032
And the Director of Nurses thinks it's a good idea.

00:19:45.033 --> 00:19:51.799
She suggests a transfer to the operating room.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:20:02.900
At one time when you were in training, you 
seriously considered making this your specialty.

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:08.000
And in the course of your work here, you discover 
something you hadn't thought of before.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.366
The citizens of this community, which is the 
sanatorium,

00:20:11.366 --> 00:20:15.666
have the same surgical needs as the citizens of 
any small town.

00:20:15.666 --> 00:20:20.060
On any one operating day, you are likely to find 
yourself assisting in a long, complicated

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:27.600
thoracoplasty one hour, and an emergency appendectomy the next.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:52.360
[Music]

00:20:52.640 --> 00:21:02.560
And little boys everywhere, even in a sanatorium, occasionally have to have their tonsils removed.

00:21:02.566 --> 00:21:06.466
You find your next move from the operating room to postoperative nursing,

00:21:06.466 --> 00:21:15.999
an easy and natural step to make.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:21.366
And you discover that nursing involves teaching, 
too.

00:21:21.366 --> 00:21:26.500
This inquisitive young lady wants to know how her operation, which involved the removal

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:37.460
of two of her ribs, is going to help arrest her tuberculosis.

00:21:37.466 --> 00:21:42.132
And so you find yourself explaining the importance of rest and how various operations,

00:21:42.133 --> 00:21:53.533
including the one she had, immobilize the lungs and give them a chance to heal.

00:21:53.533 --> 00:21:58.699
The teaching role is particularly important when 
you start to work with the children.

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:10.033
[Music]

00:22:10.033 --> 00:22:14.399
This isn't just an ordinary scolding for having dirty 
little hands.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:21.466
This is an opportunity to explain and set early in 
their lives good health habits.

00:22:21.466 --> 00:22:25.520
Your horizons as a nurse continue to expand.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:35.020
[Music]

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:40.560
You'll begin to understand the needs of the 
children and their dependence on you.

00:22:40.566 --> 00:22:44.366
[Music]

00:22:44.366 --> 00:22:49.432
And this leads you to a deeper understanding of 
the problems of the adult with tuberculosis.

00:22:49.433 --> 00:22:53.433
You begin to understand how much these people 
need you.

00:22:53.433 --> 00:23:00.499
Even in the last stages of their cure, when they 
finally reach camp, they need you more than ever.

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.466
When they first came here, they were utterly 
dependent.

00:23:03.466 --> 00:23:06.299
They had to be even when they didn't want to be.

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:16.166
That's one of the factors in a successful cure.

00:23:16.166 --> 00:23:23.932
Ed must now be taught how to do things for himself 
again and how to protect himself while doing them.

00:23:23.933 --> 00:23:33.933
[Music]

00:23:33.933 --> 00:23:38.266
You explain how they must all adapt and control their everyday actions,

00:23:38.266 --> 00:23:43.832
so that when they leave here, they will never have to come back.

00:23:43.833 --> 00:23:47.699
You are a teacher...

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.600
and always a pupil.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:54.833
You never stop learning.

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:56.566
You never stand still.

00:23:56.566 --> 00:24:02.766
You're on the move.

00:24:02.766 --> 00:24:05.966
And one day, you come back to Ward D.

00:24:05.966 --> 00:24:08.199
It's like coming back home.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:12.200
This is where it all started.

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:18.200
Most of the faces are different.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.760
And you feel different, too.

00:24:21.766 --> 00:24:24.866
You feel sure this is where you belong.

00:24:24.866 --> 00:24:27.599
The difference is that now you know some of the 
answers...

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:32.866
know them well enough to be able to help the new  nurse on your floor get started on her first year.

00:24:32.866 --> 00:24:35.032
[Music]

00:24:35.033 --> 00:24:38.699
[Buzzer]

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:45.800
[Music]

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:51.033
Even more important is the sure knowledge that 
you are capable of finding new answers

00:24:51.033 --> 00:24:57.566
for new problems.

00:24:57.566 --> 00:25:07.166
[Ed:] Hey, Miss Wallace. I've come to say goodbye.

00:25:07.166 --> 00:25:13.132
[Narrator:] In a tuberculosis sanatorium, "goodbye" 
is a happy word.

00:25:13.133 --> 00:25:20.033
Goodbye is a reward.

00:25:20.033 --> 00:25:22.933
And going home is like a song.

00:25:22.933 --> 00:25:26.299
Something of you, Nurse Wallace, goes home with 
everyone.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:26:01.500
[Music]

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:08.200
Goodbyes are important,

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.333
but there's work to do, Nurse Wallace.

00:26:11.333 --> 00:26:14.566
TB Nurse Wallace.

00:26:14.566 --> 00:26:19.440
[Music]

